  story_input: Project: atlas-small
- Schema Validation and Type Safety (2 FRs): Ensure that schema fields are properly typed and validated at lint/compile time before reaching the LLM, preventing silent failures and empty results in constrained decoding.
- Error Handling and Observability (3 FRs): Make error handling explicit and observable throughout the framework by establishing clear defaults, failing loudly on issues, and providing documented strategies for LLM node failures.
Status histogram: implemented 2, enforced 1, judged 1, approved 1
  story: {'story': "Atlas-small is a framework built around the principle that type safety and schema validation must happen before an LLM ever sees a request. The project centers on two core features that ensure schema fields are properly typed and validated at lint and compile time, catching silent failures and empty results in constrained decoding before they propagate through the system. This preventive approach treats validation as a first-class concern, not an afterthought, making the contract between the schema and the LLM explicit and verifiable.\n\nThe project's work has been driven by a parallel commitment to error handling and observability. Three features across this domain establish clear defaults, make failures loud rather than silent, and provide documented strategies for when LLM nodes encounter problems. Of the five total features in the project, two have been implemented, one has been enforced into the system, one has been judged against requirements, and one has been approved for inclusion. This distribution shows a framework moving from foundational work toward integration and validation, with error handling becoming as deliberate as the type safety that precedes it.\n\nFor newcomers, the entry point is understanding how schema validation prevents downstream failures: start with the two features under Schema Validation and Type Safety to see how the framework catches problems at compile time. Then move to the Error Handling and Observability features to learn how the framework surfaces and documents what happens when validation passes but execution encounters real-world LLM behavior. Together, these five features form a coherent story about building reliability into a system that must bridge the gap between structured schemas and the inherent uncertainty of language model outputs."}
